ot just in the  East but across the country  exactly I feel in fact you know here in  the river in the west of the country and  yet uh daily almost we do have air  strikes those extracts they are here in  the wave last week seven people died 13  people were injured because they are  military infrastructures that are of  interest for Russian forces  unfortunately uh missiles also fall on  civilians as was the case last week  there are such infrastructures that are  targeted by the Russians and also  Railway infrastructures which are of  interest for Russia because of course  Russia  imagines that weapons especially weapons  coming from western western allies will  circulate through the country either by  Road or by Railways so the efforts of  course are focused at the moment on on  eastern Ukraine when it comes to heavy  bombing a constant shelling in donbassy  in Donetsk on Harrison harkive Odessa  but the rest of the country is equally  under threat and many times a day you  would hear the sirens here and you would  have to take shelter until the end of  the alert  as we saw in your report this is taking  its toll on people's mental health and  tell us more about how people in  affected areas are reacting  well uh Phil I'll start by giving you a  figure in the region in the province of  luhansk that was initially before the  war at 350  000 inhabitants there's only 60 000  inhabitants left 290  000 people have fled they have fled  towards Western Ukraine they have fled  abroad in neighboring countries Poland  Romania Germany Slovakia that is to give  you an idea just how massive The Exodus  of all those people is in total there's  over 7 million displaced people within  Ukraine and over 5 million refugees in  neighboring countries and for those who  stay for example in the city of her son  in the south of the country well today  people were practicing in the city  center against uh Russian authorities  who have seized the city who have  established a Russian Administration in  the city they were protesting peacefully  those citizens while they were dispersed  with tear gas with stunned grenades as  well there were four civilians being  injured so people in those affected  regions are not accepting this Russian  occupation and today I was in a shelter  I met with some people from busha  Butcher and and harkiv and they those  were men those were able men and they  came here they want to train and they  want to come back to defend their cities  against the Russian Invader right now  the U.N Secretary General has now  arrived in Kiev what can we expect from  his talks with president zelanski  well Antonio guterres had met previously  on Tuesday of Vladimir Putin and he was  hoping that Russia would would let the  United Nations take part to a  humanitarian effort to evacuate  civilians who are still trapped in the  west affected areas and not least the  other steel plant in mariopol but here  authorities in Kiev are very skeptical  that any humanitarian Corridor could be  opened we've seen talks stalling over  the past a few weeks with even ngos  saying that probably what would be more  efficient than any negotiation towards a  humanitarian Corridor was would be a  real ceasefire of 24 48 72 hours to a  lot of civilians to to go away to take  shelter where they want and not to  follow a specific route route given by  the authorities  thank you for that Emmanuel Emmanuel  Shaz in levieve  so let's take a closer look at what  Vladimir Putin means when he you're  warned against it countries intervening  in moscow's war in Ukraine here he is  talking to Russian lawmakers today  if anyone from outside moves to  interfere in the current developments  and creates unacceptable strategic  threats for Russia  they should know that our response to  encounter these assaults will be  lightning fast  it will be quick  we have all the tools for this  the kind that no one else can boast of  right now  foreign  [Music]  Crump he's a CEO of global risk analysis  consultancy at sibling and a former  British tank commander welcome to DW is  President Putin stating the obvious  interfere and will come after you or is  there more to today's statement  I mean this is not the first time that  Russians have made statements along  these lines of course they've raised  this numerous times since the start of  the conflict and there's a repeat each  time of  a reaction to to the Western Supply of  weapons now that Western Supply weapons  is becoming more obvious you know  there's larger equipment being provided  there's more signature equipment being  provided it's no longer just defensive  so I'm certain that Russia is  increasingly being frustrated by this  but they're still responding by strikes  within Ukraine they haven't escalated  the conflict yet outside of Ukraine  um but I think the rhetoric will  eventually have to be followed by action  and maybe that's what we're seeing with  the imposition of restrictions on Poland  and Bulgaria in regard to gas it  potentially operations in cyberspace in  other areas where Russia can start to  extend the conflict and and express its  continued discontent the fact that this  support for Ukraine is of course helping  to hinder Russia's progress on the  battlefield the extension of this  conflict is is the interesting thing  isn't it because now we have these  mysterious explosions in moldova's  russia-backed Breakaway region that's  transnation which borders Ukraine on the  west and of course people are worried  now that this war is spilling over  um is that likely this spillover  I think you know again there's a very  good point that we don't need to  speculate too much around exactly what's  happening in transition for fear of of  starting rumors but I think it is worth  looking at how disorder and transnister  you know would benefit a different size  and I think clearly Russia benefits the  most from this and it does fit the  Russian Playbook of what happened in  Donetsk and luhansk  um you know a state of emergency was  generated that allowed mobilization and  right now for Russia being able to  operate forces from transistorian in  more Force than it currently has as you  said in the report 1500 soldiers at the  moment in transistria that's not enough  to move against Odessa or or even  pretend to move against Odessa  um but a few more forces mobilization  transistor Justified cause for  operations from there that supports  Russia's War aim that as declared  earlier in the week of moving towards  taking all of Southern Ukraine and for  now most importantly it allows Russia to  present enough of a threat to Odessa and  potentially to Michele as from the West  that it draws off Ukrainian forces from  the donbass it stops Ukraine from  reinforcing the main fight against  Russia so if you have to look at which  side benefits more from this it's  definitely Russia in the way it sits and  so I think suspicion is obviously going  to point that way that this is an  escalation to support Russia's War girls  just shifting the topics slightly I'm  intrigued by yesterday's meeting of  40-yard countries here in Germany  chaired by the U.S defense secretary  these are going to become a monthly  meetings to coordinate weapon supplies  to Ukraine  what do you think that Ukraine needs now  that the West isn't already providing  this is a short of of of air defense  cover  um what else would prove decisive in  this war  well and you mentioned  um aerial support and the rebuilding of  the Ukrainian Air Force and actually  some work has gone into spares for that  and uh Liz truss the UK foreign  secretary actually has now publicly said  we should be providing um actually  aircraft to Ukraine which was a red flag  in the conflict two months ago and I  think the fact we were even now having a  monthly donors conference shows how far  thinking has moved on from the early  days when the Assumption was that  Ukraine would be fighting an Insurgency  after a couple of weeks that Russia  would have taken much of the country and  it was going to be a different sort of  resistance what we're actually seeing  with full-fledged fighting uh in eastern  Ukraine in southern Ukraine and hence  that change in supplies you know Germany  now supplying anti-aircraft um armored  vehicles which are very effective  against drones low-flying helicopters  low-flying aircraft the sort of threats  we're seeing  um tanks being supplied to the point the  Ukraine maybe has more tanks now than it  started the conflict with uh which given  two months of fighting is astonishing  really and Russia certainly can't say  the same  um and I think you're going to see more  more focus on these War fighting  supplies rather than defensive Aid  rather than things that help in  Insurgency and I think that'll be  sustained but the most important thing  right now is artillery and well over 150  NATO caliber artillery pieces now  pledged to Ukraine with supporting  ammunition and Bulgaria supplying  soviet-era ammunition maybe one reason  that Bulgaria has been targeted and  picked out for these oil and sorry the  gas sanctions so that's how it's going  to keep developing that's very clear  thank you so much for that Justin Crump  from risk consultancy assembly

